WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.950
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:10.570
In the film industry, we can often see footage
of a human walking on the Moon, fighting underwater,

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.480
or appearing in any environment without actually
going there.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.620
To do this, a piece of footage of the actor
is recorded in front of a green screen, and

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.410
then, the background of the scene is changed
to something else.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:28.310
This process is called green-screen keying,
and in theory, this sounds simple enough,

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.600
but make no mistake - this is a challenging
problem.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.760
Here's why.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:38.149
Issue number one is that separating the foreground
from the background is non-trivial and is

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:40.179
not a fully automatic process.

00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:45.910
Let's call this semi-automatic because the
compositing artist starts drawing these separation

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:50.370
masks, and even though there is some help
from pre-existing software, it still takes

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.190
quite a bit of manual labor.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:58.120
For instance, in this example, it is extremely
difficult to create a perfect separation between

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.519
the background and the hair of the actor.

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:06.000
Our eyes are extremely keen on catching such
details, so even the slightest inaccuracies

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.790
are going to appear as glaring mistakes.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:13.290
This takes a ton of time and effort from the
side of the artist, and we haven't even talked

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.790
about tracking the changes between frames
as we're talking about video animations.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.800
I think it is now easy to see that this is
a hugely relevant problem in the post production

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.240
of feature films.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.360
And now, on to issue number two, which is
subtracting indirect illumination from this

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.360
footage.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.980
This is a beautiful light transport effect
where the color of different diffuse objects

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.770
bleed onto each other.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:41.410
In this case, the green color of the background
bleeds onto the karate uniform.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:46.510
That is normally a beautiful effect, but here,
it is highly undesirable because if we put

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.870
this character in a different environment,
it won't look like it belongs there.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:55.590
It will look more like one of those super
fake Photoshop disasters that we see everywhere

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.590
on the internet.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.520
And this technique offers a novel solution
to this keying problem.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:06.190
First, we are asked to scribble on the screen
and mark the most dominant colors of the scene.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:11.200
This we only have to do once even though we're
processing an entire video.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.120
As a result, we get an initial map where we
can easily fix some of the issues.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.959
This is very easy and intuitive, not like
those long sessions spent with pixel-by-pixel

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:22.959
editing.

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:26.629
These colors are then propagated to the entirety
of the animation.

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:32.359
The final results are compared to a ton of
already existing methods on the market and

00:02:32.359 --> 00:02:33.940
this one smokes them all.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:39.370
However, what is even more surprising is that
it is also way better than what an independent

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:42.989
artist produced which took ten times that
long.

00:02:42.989 --> 00:02:47.989
Similar comparisons are also made for removing
indirect illumination, which is also referred

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:50.219
to as color unmixing in the paper.

00:02:50.219 --> 00:02:55.870
It is also shown that the algorithm is not
too sensitive to this choice of dominant colors,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.999
so there is room for amazing followup papers
to make the process a bit more automatic.

00:03:00.999 --> 00:03:04.960
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

